@Tutorial(time: 30) {
    @Intro(title: "Build a Coding Agent") {
        A surprising amount of an "AI coding assistant" is just a model, a few file tools, and a
        loop. Having built a one-tool agent in <doc:BuildAnAgent>, you'll now build a real one:
        an agent that can list, read, and edit files in a directory to carry out tasks you
        describe — then make it interactive.

        You'll need a tool-capable model (this tutorial uses OpenAI) and an `OPENAI_API_KEY` in
        your environment. Prefer to run offline first? The bundled `codingAgent` example does the
        whole thing with a scripted model: `swift run ShikishaExamples codingAgent`.

        @Image(source: "codingagent-intro.png", alt: "A robot wired to read/list, edit, run, and filesystem tools beside a code editor, on a dark amber background")
    }

    @Section(title: "Give the agent eyes: read_file") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            ``ToolCallingAgent`` already provides the loop (call → execute → observe → repeat).
            Your job is to give the model useful tools and a sandbox to work in. Start with a
            single read-only tool.

            One thing that's easy to miss: a tool-capable model must be constructed **with** the
            tool specs (`tools: tools.map { $0.toOpenAISpec() }`) so it can *emit* tool calls.
            The agent only *executes* them — it doesn't attach the specs for you.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Define a sandbox `root` and a `read_file` tool with ``TypedTool``, which decodes
                the arguments into a `Decodable` struct.

                @Code(name: "CodingAgent.swift", file: "codeagent-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Build the model **with** the tool specs, create a ``ToolCallingAgent`` with the
                same tools, and run a question. The model can now read files to answer.

                @Code(name: "CodingAgent.swift", file: "codeagent-01.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Let it explore: list_files") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Reading a known file is useful, but the agent should be able to discover what's there.
            Add a `list_files` tool. With both tools, the model can plan: list, pick a file, read
            it, then answer.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Add a `list_files` tool whose `path` argument is optional (default the project
                root). Register it alongside `read_file`.

                @Code(name: "CodingAgent.swift", file: "codeagent-02.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Ask a question that requires both tools. Watch the agent chain them across
                iterations without any extra code from you.

                @Code(name: "CodingAgent.swift", file: "codeagent-02.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Give it hands: edit_file") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Now make the agent *act*. A single `edit_file` tool — replace `old_str` with
            `new_str`, or create the file when `old_str` is empty — is enough to write new files
            and patch existing ones. This is the move that turns a question-answerer into a
            coding agent.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Add `edit_file`. The `EditFileArgs` struct maps the snake_case JSON keys
                (`old_str`, `new_str`) the model emits onto Swift properties via `CodingKeys`.

                @Code(name: "CodingAgent.swift", file: "codeagent-03.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Ask the agent to create a file. It will call `edit_file` with an empty `old_str`
                and the file's contents — and the file appears on disk.

                @Code(name: "CodingAgent.swift", file: "codeagent-03.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Make it a conversation") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Finally, wrap the agent in a read-eval-print loop with ``BufferMemory`` so it
            remembers earlier turns, and add a ``ConsoleCallback`` so you can watch it think. Now
            you can give follow-up instructions like "now add a test for it."
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Add memory and a console callback, then loop over `readLine()`. Each turn,
                append the user's message, run the agent over the full history, and store the
                reply for context.

                @Code(name: "CodingAgent.swift", file: "codeagent-04.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Where to take it") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            You now have a working coding agent in well under a hundred lines. Natural next steps:

            - **Safety** — keep `resolve(_:)` confined to the sandbox, and consider confirming
              edits before writing. Tools that touch the filesystem deserve guardrails.
            - **More tools** — run tests, search code, or call `git`. Each is just another
              ``Tool``.
            - **Observability** — swap in ``TracingCallback`` or a ``CostTrackerCallback`` to log
              every step and track spend (see <doc:Observability>).
            - **Control the loop** — for plan/act/reflect cycles or retries, drive the steps
              yourself with a ``StateGraph`` (see <doc:Graphs>) instead of ``ToolCallingAgent``.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Run the offline version end to end to see the loop, the tool calls, and the file
                it writes — no API key required.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: "codeagent-run.txt")
            }
        }
    }
}
